Subject: [PATCH v5 0/2] mm: batch file VMA insertions during fork
To: linux-mm@kvack.org
Cc: linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org,
    linux-kselftest@vger.kernel.org,
    Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>,
    David Hildenbrand <david@kernel.org>,
    "Liam R. Howlett" <liam@infradead.org>,
    Lorenzo Stoakes <ljs@kernel.org>,
    Shuah Khan <shuah@kernel.org>,
    Mateusz Guzik <mjguzik@gmail.com>,
    Pedro Falcato <pfalcato@suse.de>,
    Yibin Liu <liuyibin@hygon.cn>

dup_mmap() acquires the file mapping's i_mmap_rwsem separately for each
inherited VMA. This series groups up to eight eligible VMAs for the same
address_space into one insertion critical section. A full batch therefore
uses one lock acquisition instead of eight.

The first patch shares the existing page-table copy predicate and adds
batching. Pending entries are inserted before copying a file VMA's page
tables, and any remainder is drained at loop_out before successful
completion or failed-fork teardown. The second patch adds rmap tests for
fork, truncation and write sealing.

A related RFC proposed batching file VMA insertions:
https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260616091302.2725675-1-liuyibin@hygon.cn/
Pedro Falcato pointed out the race between copying file PTEs and delayed
rmap insertion:
https://lore.kernel.org/all/ajEfJyYRbhVo4dFK@pedro-suse.lan/
This series flushes before copying file page tables, using the same
vma_needs_copy() predicate as copy_page_range().

Changes since v4:
  - Remove the has_open flag and its extra batch flush. Preserve the open
    callback itself, before the current file VMA is queued.
  - Only flush before page-table copying when the current VMA has a file.
    Copying a VMA without a file does not need to interrupt the batch.
  - Restore arch_dup_mmap() to its original position before loop_out.
    Drain the remaining batch at loop_out on success and on either an
    iteration error or an architecture initialization error.
  - Keep the upstream selftest patch unchanged. Add a project diagnostic
    for the data and attachment lifetime of 33 SysV SHM mappings, and
    trace callback execution and batch processing during fork.

Validation on x86_64 QEMU TCG with KASAN and PROVE_LOCKING:
  - Full kernel build and strict checkpatch: passed.
  - Fork/rmap tests: 10 passed.
  - Guard-region fork cases: 5 passed, 4 inapplicable variants skipped.
  - 32 forced fork ENOMEM failures: cleanup and write sealing passed.
  - Trace of 30 forks: all 202 observed file VMA copy_page_range() calls
    followed completed insertion of the same destination VMA.
  - Batch tracing: four full batches for 33 same-file VMAs and 32
    seven-entry tail batches during forced fork failures.
  - SysV SHM: data and attachment lifetime passed; the trace recorded
    33 shm_open() calls and four full eight-entry batches.
  - No KASAN, lockdep or other kernel diagnostics were observed.

These functional and ordering checks do not establish a latency benefit.
The architecture hook review includes the generic, arm64, powerpc and
x86 implementations, including Xen page-table pinning; runtime testing
was limited to native x86_64. USERFAULTFD was disabled in the test config.

The batching change is based on Dong Chenchen's supplied prototype.
Codex assisted with adaptation, tests and descriptions, as recorded by
the Assisted-by tags.

Dong Chenchen (2):
  mm: batch file VMA insertions in dup_mmap
  selftests/mm: cover file VMA lifetime across fork

